Reader bands:

7
00:00:13.932 --> 00:00:16.215
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

8
00:00:16.495 --> 00:00:38.041
Ch 03 (HTTP, CDNs), Ch 07 (caching, invalidation), Ch 10 (replication), What you will build: the storage and delivery path for every file on the platform: product photos and videos from sellers, invoice PDFs, KYC documents, delivery-proof photos, bulk price files, data-lake files.

9
00:00:38.321 --> 00:00:51.335
It covers how an object store keeps data durable without three full copies, how large uploads survive bad networks, how images become the right size for each screen, and how a CDN serves them fresh.

10
00:00:51.615 --> 00:00:59.145
This is the design S3 or Dropbox or an image service interview question, applied to a grocery marketplace.

11
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:00.940
Results from the four labs:

12
00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.018
L1, erasure coding:

13
00:01:04.298 --> 00:01:22.218
A real Reed, Solomon codec destroyed 3 of 9 (and 4 of 14) shards per object, including data shards, and rebuilt 16 or 16 objects bit-for-bit (SHA-256 verified) at ~290 to 340 MB per second.

14
00:01:22.498 --> 00:01:36.514
In a durability model for 10 PB, RS(10,4) at 1.4 times storage has a lower yearly loss probability (3.3e-5) than 3 replicas at 3 times (1.3e-2).

15
00:01:36.794 --> 00:01:38.885
The cost is 10 times repair reads.

16
00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:45.095
L2, uploads (real HTTP, 0.4% drop per 64 KB):

17
00:01:46.445 --> 00:01:50.499
A 160 MB single PUT never finished in 5 tries.

18
00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:58.352
An 8 MB multipart upload finished with 9 part retries, and in 5.0 s with 4 parallel parts.

19
00:01:58.632 --> 00:02:00.808
Every object was verified intact.

20
00:02:01.088 --> 00:02:05.163
L3, image variants (real JDK resize + JPEG):

21
00:02:05.443 --> 00:02:10.200
The 300 px variant is 1.6% of the original's bytes.

22
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:17.563
Serving variants instead of originals cut a day's egress from 5.6 TB to 132 GB.

23
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:21.768
A CDN kept 92% of requests off the origin.

24
00:02:23.118 --> 00:02:27.747
On a busy catalogue, eager versus lazy variants mattered less than expected:

25
00:02:28.027 --> 00:02:31.505
97% of variants get requested within a month.

26
00:02:31.785 --> 00:02:33.641
L4, CDN freshness:

27
00:02:33.921 --> 00:02:41.046
Mutable URLs with 7-day TTLs served 81,271 outdated photos.

28
00:02:41.326 --> 00:02:45.635
5-minute TTLs multiplied origin traffic 46 times.

29
00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:47.963
Purges failed silently for days.

30
00:02:48.243 --> 00:02:55.241
Content-hashed URLs gave 99.1% hit ratio and a worst staleness of 54 s.

31
00:02:56.591 --> 00:02:57.572
