35
00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:07.751
A story: the photo of last year's packaging.

36
00:03:08.031 --> 00:03:15.199
The first media setup was a or uploads directory on two app servers, synced by rsync, behind nginx.

37
00:03:15.479 --> 00:03:16.823
This is how it went wrong:

38
00:03:17.103 --> 00:03:18.489
Seller uploads failed.

39
00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:24.508
Sellers in Tier-2 towns uploaded 200 MB catalogue videos over mobile data.

40
00:03:24.788 --> 00:03:29.695
The single POST through the app server failed at 70% and restarted from zero.

41
00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:31.831
Support tickets said upload stuck.

42
00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:34.457
The app servers ran out of disk and threads.

43
00:03:34.737 --> 00:03:36.999
Every upload tied up a worker for minutes.

44
00:03:37.279 --> 00:03:40.735
One disk filled, and uploads started failing platform-wide.

45
00:03:42.085 --> 00:03:48.229
A disk died. rsync had been failing quietly for a week, and 40,000 product photos were gone.

46
00:03:48.509 --> 00:03:50.279
Sellers were asked to re-upload.

47
00:03:50.559 --> 00:03:56.789
Phones downloaded 2400-pixel originals to show 300-pixel thumbnails.

48
00:03:57.069 --> 00:04:00.717
The app felt slow on 4G, and the CDN bill tripled.

49
00:04:00.997 --> 00:04:02.127
Stale photos.

50
00:04:02.407 --> 00:04:12.093
After moving to a CDN with a 7-day cache, a brand changed its packaging, and customers saw the old pack for a week and complained wrong product delivered.

51
00:04:13.443 --> 00:04:29.037
Files look simple, but at scale they are a durability problem (disks die, in racks, in batches), a transfer problem (large objects over bad networks), a processing problem (variants, formats, scanning) and a delivery problem (caching and freshness).

52
00:04:30.387 --> 00:04:31.070
Quote:

53
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:33.014
The thesis of this chapter.

54
00:04:33.294 --> 00:04:36.259
Durability comes from redundancy you can afford.

55
00:04:36.539 --> 00:04:44.134
Erasure coding survives more failures than triple replication at half the storage, and pays for it in repair traffic (L1).

56
00:04:44.414 --> 00:04:54.419
Large objects move in parts: multipart uploads direct to storage via pre-signed URLs, with per-part retries and end-to-end checksums (L2).

57
00:04:54.699 --> 00:05:02.635
Serve the right bytes: size and format variants made lazily or eagerly, cached in the object store and at the edge (L3).

58
00:05:02.915 --> 00:05:11.278
Never change content under a URL: versioned, content-hashed URLs make CDN caching both long and correct (L4).

