222
00:12:12.882 --> 00:12:18.173
Erasure coding splits an object into k data shards and computes m parity shards.

223
00:12:18.453 --> 00:12:19.690
Any k rebuild it.

224
00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:27.501
L1 rebuilt 16 or 16 objects after wiping data shards, verified by SHA-256.

225
00:12:27.781 --> 00:12:29.061
Repair is the price.

226
00:12:29.341 --> 00:12:33.949
Rebuilding one shard reads k, which is more network and longer repair windows.

227
00:12:34.229 --> 00:12:42.463
So stores replicate small, hot or freshly written data and erasure-code larger, colder data, often converting in the background.

228
00:12:43.813 --> 00:12:45.989
The model assumes independent failures.

229
00:12:46.269 --> 00:12:53.821
Real losses come from correlated ones (a rack, a firmware batch, a zone), so shards are spread across failure domains.

230
00:12:54.101 --> 00:13:02.336
Scrubbing (reading and verifying checksums periodically) finds silent corruption before a second failure makes it unrecoverable.

