00:20:34.762 --> 00:20:39.989
With mutable URLs, the TTL is a trade-off between freshness and origin load.

400
00:20:40.269 --> 00:20:43.853
Purges are asynchronous, rate-limited and occasionally fail.

401
00:20:44.133 --> 00:20:46.842
Versioned URLs make objects immutable.

402
00:20:47.122 --> 00:20:53.650
The API returns the current URL; old URLs keep working, and eventually age out of caches.

403
00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:58.666
Staleness is bounded by how long the referring page or API is cached.

404
00:20:58.946 --> 00:20:59.778
Headers:

405
00:21:00.058 --> 00:21:01.167
Versioned assets:

406
00:21:01.447 --> 00:21:07.911
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable.

407
00:21:09.261 --> 00:21:13.507
Pages that reference them: short TTLs or stale-while-revalidate.

408
00:21:13.787 --> 00:21:18.011
Private objects: private, no-store, served via signed URLs.

409
00:21:18.291 --> 00:21:28.232
Origin shield: a mid-tier cache in front of the origin collapses misses from many edges into one fetch, and protects the transform service from stampedes.

410
00:21:29.582 --> 00:21:31.417
Access control and privacy.

411
00:21:31.697 --> 00:21:40.635
Public product media: public via the CDN, with origin access restricted to the CDN (origin access identity or control).

412
00:21:40.915 --> 00:21:53.630
Invoices, KYC, delivery proofs: private buckets, encryption at rest with KMS keys (Ch 36), and access via short-lived signed URLs issued after an authorisation check.

413
00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:54.977
Log access.

414
00:21:55.257 --> 00:21:57.923
Strip EXIF from customer and rider photos:

415
00:21:58.203 --> 00:22:00.635
GPS coordinates are personal data.

416
00:22:00.915 --> 00:22:11.113
Retention: lifecycle rules delete delivery proofs after 90 days, and object lock (WORM) protects invoices for their legal retention period (VERIFY).

417
00:22:12.463 --> 00:22:13.871
Lifecycle and cost.

418
00:22:14.151 --> 00:22:23.644
Storage classes: standard, then infrequent access, then archive (for example, Glacier-like), with retrieval costs and delays for the colder tiers.

419
00:22:23.924 --> 00:22:26.484
Lifecycle rules move objects by age.

420
00:22:26.764 --> 00:22:29.153
Egress usually dominates for media.

421
00:22:29.433 --> 00:22:33.487
CDN offload, right-sizing and modern formats are the levers.

422
00:22:33.767 --> 00:22:39.420
Request costs: many tiny objects (thumbnails, logs) cost more in requests than in bytes.

423
00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:45.396
Batch small files (for example, Parquet), or keep variants cache-only and regenerate them.

424
00:22:46.746 --> 00:22:49.733
Abandoned multipart uploads silently cost storage.

425
00:22:50.013 --> 00:22:53.085
Add a lifecycle rule to abort them after N days.

426
00:22:54.435 --> 00:22:55.565
Interview framing.

427
00:22:55.845 --> 00:23:00.219
Design an object store or design an image hosting or upload service.

428
00:23:00.499 --> 00:23:14.451
Cover: the metadata or data split;. placement and durability (replication versus erasure coding with numbers);. consistency;. the multipart upload flow with pre-signed URLs;. the processing pipeline;.

429
00:23:14.731 --> 00:23:17.376
CDN delivery with versioned URLs;.

430
00:23:18.726 --> 00:23:21.243
access control;. lifecycle and cost.

431
00:23:21.523 --> 00:23:30.057
Staff-level signals: correlated failures and scrubbing, repair traffic, never mutate content under a URL, and egress economics.

