One big PUT over mobile networks.

745
00:45:28.117 --> 00:45:29.867
It never finishes (L2).

746
00:45:31.217 --> 00:45:32.134
Watch out:

747
00:45:32.414 --> 00:45:33.161
6.

748
00:45:33.441 --> 00:45:34.529
No checksums.

749
00:45:34.809 --> 00:45:36.025
Silent corruption.

750
00:45:37.375 --> 00:45:38.292
Watch out:

751
00:45:38.572 --> 00:45:39.319
7.

752
00:45:39.599 --> 00:45:43.076
Serving originals. ~40 times the bytes (L3).

753
00:45:44.426 --> 00:45:45.343
Watch out:

754
00:45:45.623 --> 00:45:46.306
8.

755
00:45:46.586 --> 00:45:48.186
Trusting file extensions.

756
00:45:48.466 --> 00:45:50.173
Malware as.jpg.

757
00:45:51.523 --> 00:45:52.440
Watch out:

758
00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:53.467
9.

759
00:45:53.747 --> 00:45:56.200
Mutable URLs with long TTLs.

760
00:45:56.480 --> 00:45:58.613
Days of stale content (L4).

761
00:45:59.963 --> 00:46:00.881
Watch out:

762
00:46:01.161 --> 00:46:01.865
10.

763
00:46:02.145 --> 00:46:04.129
Freshness that depends on purges.

764
00:46:04.409 --> 00:46:06.393
They fail silently (L4).

765
00:46:07.743 --> 00:46:08.660
Watch out:

766
00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:09.772
11.

767
00:46:10.052 --> 00:46:12.377
Public links to invoices and KYC.

768
00:46:12.657 --> 00:46:13.724
Data breach.

769
00:46:15.074 --> 00:46:15.991
Watch out:

770
00:46:16.271 --> 00:46:17.061
12.

771
00:46:17.341 --> 00:46:18.791
No lifecycle rules.

772
00:46:19.071 --> 00:46:21.653
Storage and abandoned uploads grow forever.

773
00:46:23.003 --> 00:46:23.877
Part 8.

774
00:46:24.157 --> 00:46:25.501
Interview Q and A.

775
00:46:26.751 --> 00:46:27.583
Beginner.

776
00:46:27.863 --> 00:46:28.887
Question 1.

777
00:46:29.167 --> 00:46:32.751
Design an image upload and hosting service, main components?

778
00:46:33.031 --> 00:46:34.397
Thirty-second answer:

779
00:46:34.677 --> 00:46:56.607
An API that authorises uploads and issues pre-signed URLs; clients upload directly (multipart) to object storage; an event-driven pipeline validates, scans, strips metadata and makes variants; the catalogue stores versioned URLs; a CDN with an origin shield serves images; private objects use signed URLs.

780
00:46:57.957 --> 00:46:58.960
Question 2.

781
00:46:59.240 --> 00:47:02.013
How do large uploads survive bad networks?

782
00:47:02.293 --> 00:47:03.659
Thirty-second answer:

783
00:47:03.939 --> 00:47:21.005
Multipart uploads: split into parts, upload in parallel, retry failed parts only, verify per-part and final checksums, assemble on complete; persist the upload id to resume after app restarts; abort abandoned uploads with lifecycle rules.

784
00:47:22.355 --> 00:47:23.401
Question 3.

785
00:47:23.681 --> 00:47:25.579
Why not serve the original image?

786
00:47:25.859 --> 00:47:27.225
Thirty-second answer:

787
00:47:27.505 --> 00:47:40.134
Screens show 80 to 600 px; a 2400 px original is 30 to 400 times the bytes of the displayed size; it slows the app, costs egress and battery.

788
00:47:40.414 --> 00:47:43.102
Serve right-sized variants in modern formats.

789
00:47:44.452 --> 00:47:45.519
Question 4.

790
00:47:45.799 --> 00:47:47.505
What is a pre-signed URL?

791
00:47:47.785 --> 00:47:49.151
Thirty-second answer:

792
00:47:49.431 --> 00:48:05.409
A URL containing a signature over the method, key, expiry (and optionally headers like content type and length), created with the server's credentials, that lets a client perform exactly that operation directly on the object store for a short time.

793
00:48:06.759 --> 00:48:07.762
Intermediate.

794
00:48:08.042 --> 00:48:09.151
Question 5.

795
00:48:09.431 --> 00:48:11.629
Replication versus erasure coding?

796
00:48:11.909 --> 00:48:13.274
Thirty-second answer:

797
00:48:13.554 --> 00:48:31.197
Replication stores full copies (3 times for two failures) and repairs by copying one replica; erasure coding stores k data + m parity shards (1.4 times for RS(10,4), surviving four failures) but repairs by reading k shards.

798
00:48:31.477 --> 00:48:37.535
Use replication for small or hot or fresh data, erasure coding for large or colder data.

799
00:48:38.885 --> 00:48:39.995
Question 6.

800
00:48:40.275 --> 00:48:42.429
How do you keep CDN content fresh?

801
00:48:42.709 --> 00:48:44.075
Thirty-second answer:

802
00:48:44.355 --> 00:48:57.688
Make content immutable per URL: version URLs by content hash, cache for a year with immutable, and change the URL when content changes; keep the referring API or page TTL short.

803
00:48:57.968 --> 00:49:00.933
Use purges for takedowns, not normal updates.

804
00:49:02.283 --> 00:49:03.371
Question 7.

805
00:49:03.651 --> 00:49:06.403
How do you secure private documents like invoices?

806
00:49:06.683 --> 00:49:08.049
Thirty-second answer:

807
00:49:08.329 --> 00:49:21.875
Private bucket with public access blocked, encryption with KMS, access only through short-lived signed URLs issued after an authorisation check, access logging, retention and object lock where required.

808
00:49:23.225 --> 00:49:24.249
Question 8.

809
00:49:24.529 --> 00:49:26.684
What does a media processing pipeline do?

810
00:49:26.964 --> 00:49:28.329
Thirty-second answer:

811
00:49:28.609 --> 00:49:44.993
Validates type and size, scans for malware, moderates content, strips EXIF, deduplicates by hash, extracts metadata, makes variants or transcodes, and publishes versioned URLs, event-driven, idempotent, with quarantine before publication.

812
00:49:46.343 --> 00:49:47.410
Question 9.

813
00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:49.845
How do lifecycle rules save money?

814
00:49:50.125 --> 00:49:51.490
Thirty-second answer:

815
00:49:51.770 --> 00:50:04.293
They move objects to cheaper classes as they age (infrequent, archive), delete them at the end of retention, and abort abandoned multipart uploads; they encode the retention policy as data.

816
00:50:05.643 --> 00:50:06.411
Senior.

817
00:50:06.691 --> 00:50:07.736
Question 10.

818
00:50:08.016 --> 00:50:10.128
Design an object store like S3.

819
00:50:10.408 --> 00:50:11.773
Thirty-second answer:

820
00:50:12.053 --> 00:50:34.368
A front-end API tier (auth, routing); a strongly consistent sharded metadata service (bucket per key or version, then placement, checksums, ACL); data nodes storing chunks, replicated then erasure-coded across racks and zones; background repair, rebalancing, scrubbing and lifecycle; multipart upload support; events for notifications.

821
00:50:35.718 --> 00:50:36.849
Question 11.

822
00:50:37.129 --> 00:50:39.049
Eager or lazy image variants?

823
00:50:39.329 --> 00:50:40.694
Thirty-second answer:

824
00:50:40.974 --> 00:50:51.235
Eager costs CPU and storage for every size of every upload; lazy costs latency on the first request and needs a transform service behind the CDN.

825
00:50:51.515 --> 00:50:58.897
With heavy traffic nearly all variants get requested anyway; lazy wins for sparse catalogues and adding new sizes.

826
00:50:59.177 --> 00:51:02.398
Common: eager for top sizes, lazy for the rest.

827
00:51:03.748 --> 00:51:04.836
Question 12.

828
00:51:05.116 --> 00:51:08.337
How do correlated failures change durability design?

829
00:51:08.617 --> 00:51:09.983
Thirty-second answer:

830
00:51:10.263 --> 00:51:26.903
Independent-failure models underestimate risk; spread replicas or shards across racks, power domains and zones; stagger firmware and hardware batches; scrub to detect latent errors; keep repair fast; consider cross-region copies for critical data.

831
00:51:28.253 --> 00:51:29.533
Question 13.

832
00:51:29.813 --> 00:51:32.117
How do you deduplicate uploaded images?

833
00:51:32.397 --> 00:51:33.762
Thirty-second answer:

834
00:51:34.042 --> 00:51:51.087
Exact duplicates via content hash (SHA-256) as the storage key; near-duplicates via perceptual hashes (pHash or dHash) with a Hamming-distance threshold, used for catalogue quality (same product listed twice) rather than storage.

835
00:51:52.437 --> 00:51:53.632
Question 14.

836
00:51:53.912 --> 00:51:57.005
What do an origin shield and request coalescing do?

837
00:51:57.285 --> 00:51:58.651
Thirty-second answer:

838
00:51:58.931 --> 00:52:05.139
The shield is a central cache tier between edges and origin; on a miss, many edges share one fetch.

839
00:52:05.419 --> 00:52:11.499
Coalescing makes concurrent requests for the same missing object wait for a single origin fetch.

840
00:52:11.779 --> 00:52:16.109
Together they protect origins and transform services from stampedes.

841
00:52:17.459 --> 00:52:18.355
Architect.

842
00:52:18.635 --> 00:52:19.830
Question 15.

843
00:52:20.110 --> 00:52:23.331
Design the media platform for a marketplace end to end.

844
00:52:23.611 --> 00:52:24.977
Thirty-second answer:

845
00:52:25.257 --> 00:52:49.321
Managed erasure-coded object storage with versioning; pre-signed multipart uploads into quarantine; event-driven pipeline (scan, strip, dedup, variants, transcode); versioned URLs in the catalogue; CDN with shield and on-the-fly transforms; private classes with KMS and signed URLs; lifecycle tiers; observability of uploads, pipeline and delivery.

846
00:52:50.671 --> 00:52:51.865
Question 16.

847
00:52:52.145 --> 00:52:54.407
When would you self-host object storage?

848
00:52:54.687 --> 00:52:56.052
Thirty-second answer:

849
00:52:56.332 --> 00:53:11.756
When egress or storage pricing dominates at massive scale, data residency or on-prem requirements apply, or special performance is needed, with MinIO or Ceph and a team to operate durability, repair, upgrades and security.

850
00:53:12.036 --> 00:53:14.852
Otherwise managed storage is cheaper in total.

851
00:53:16.202 --> 00:53:17.546
Question 17.

852
00:53:17.826 --> 00:53:19.767
How do you design for egress cost?

853
00:53:20.047 --> 00:53:21.413
Thirty-second answer:

854
00:53:21.693 --> 00:53:36.455
CDN offload with high hit ratios (versioned immutable URLs), right-sized variants, modern formats, compression, cache-friendly URL design, regional origins, and monitoring bytes per view by client and feature.

855
00:53:37.805 --> 00:53:38.979
Question 18.

856
00:53:39.259 --> 00:53:42.075
How do you handle content moderation and takedowns?

857
00:53:42.355 --> 00:53:43.720
Thirty-second answer:

858
00:53:44.000 --> 00:54:01.685
Automated scanning (malware, prohibited content, counterfeit or brand signals) in quarantine, human review queues for borderline cases, fast takedown path that deletes origins, purges CDN caches and removes references, with an audit trail and legal hold support.

859
00:54:03.035 --> 00:54:04.273
Question 19.

860
00:54:04.553 --> 00:54:07.241
How does the data lake use object storage well?

861
00:54:07.521 --> 00:54:08.886
Thirty-second answer:

862
00:54:09.166 --> 00:54:27.214
Large columnar files (Parquet 128 MB, 1 GB) rather than many tiny ones; partitioned by date and key; table formats (Iceberg or Delta or Hudi) for transactions, schema evolution and compaction; lifecycle to colder tiers; request costs matter.

863
00:54:28.564 --> 00:54:29.673
Question 20.

864
00:54:29.953 --> 00:54:32.449
What are the health metrics of a media platform?

865
00:54:32.729 --> 00:54:34.095
Thirty-second answer:

866
00:54:34.375 --> 00:54:53.084
Upload completion rate and time; pipeline lag and rejection rates; CDN and shield hit ratios; bytes per view; image latency at the edge; stale-content incidents; storage growth by class; abandoned uploads; scrub or repair status; private-object access anomalies.

867
00:54:54.434 --> 00:54:55.330
Part 9.

868
00:54:55.610 --> 00:54:57.018
Summary and Practice.

869
00:54:58.268 --> 00:54:59.356
Key takeaways.

870
00:54:59.636 --> 00:55:06.847
Erasure coding gives more durability than replication at less than half the storage, and pays in repair traffic (L1).

871
00:55:07.127 --> 00:55:11.351
Multipart, direct, checksummed uploads survive bad networks (L2).

872
00:55:11.631 --> 00:55:13.380
Right-size and cache images.

873
00:55:13.660 --> 00:55:15.943
That is where the bytes and the bill are (L3).

874
00:55:16.223 --> 00:55:21.193
Versioned, immutable URLs make CDN caching long and correct (L4).

875
00:55:21.473 --> 00:55:26.380
Private data stays behind signed URLs, KMS and lifecycle rules.

876
